Subject: [PATCH] =?UTF-8?q?fix(domain):=20the=20old=20site=20is=20live=20a?=
 =?UTF-8?q?nd=20404ing=20=E2=80=94=20the=20executable=20fix,=20for=20whate?=
 =?UTF-8?q?ver=20is=20hosting=20it?=
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

verify-domain-redirect.mjs ran with real egress and returned the state nobody
had measured: www.ecowoodshardwood.com/ answers 200 — a second live homepage for
this business — and every deep path answers 404.

That is worse than either failure alone. A second homepage competes with
ecowoods.ca for the entity, which is the exact split this work exists to end and
it has been running the whole time. Every other path sends a person to a dead
page and a crawler to nothing. The redirects from F-196 cannot help: they fire
only if the domain resolves to this app, and it resolves to whatever serves that
200.

old-domain/ is the fix and it does not assume the host: .htaccess, nginx.conf,
_redirects, vercel.json, and index.php as a last resort. EXECUTE.md opens with
two curl commands that identify the stack, a table mapping the answer to the
right file, and separate paths for Cloudflare (a rule, no hosting needed) and
for Wix/Squarespace where no uploaded file works at all and the DNS must move.

The supplied .htaccess had the classic bug: forcing HTTPS first and redirecting
the host second gives http://www.old/x -> https://www.old/x ->
https://ecowoods.ca/x, two hops where one was needed. The version here has no
HTTPS-forcing rule on purpose. nginx.conf calls out the same trap plus
$request_uri vs $uri, which silently drops every query string, and the expired
certificate that kills a finished migration a year later because TLS fails
before any redirect can be sent.

The verifier now reports what is serving the old domain, because once it answers
200 the next question is always which config applies.

Also separates two questions that should never have been merged. Wiring the
old-domain check into verify-live.sh as a hard failure made every deploy print
"the deploy is not correct" for a different domain on different infrastructure.
That is how a red light stops meaning anything, and verify-live is the only
check here that can see a delivery failure. It WARNs loudly now and leaves the
verdict alone; pnpm verify:domain stays the hard gate.

GBP runbook imported with every figure unhardcoded — counts read from
REVIEW_EVIDENCE, prices from lib/pricing.ts, Q&A seeded from the commercial
pages in the same words.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
